{"url": "https://www.utep.edu/miningminds/", "title": "Mining Minds", "text": "(function(d, s, id) {\n  var js, fjs = d.getElementsByTagName(s)[0];\n  if (d.getElementById(id)) return;\n  js = d.createElement(s); js.id = id;\n  js.src = \"//connect.facebook.net/en_US/sdk.js#xfbml=1&version=v2.7\";\n  fjs.parentNode.insertBefore(js, fjs);\n}(document, 'script', 'facebook-jssdk')); Skip to main content MinerAlert Search pages and people Toggle navigation Search Search pages and people My UTEP Resources for INFORMATION FOR: Students Faculty & Staff Alumni Parents Quick Links X Quick Links Library Parking & Maps UTEP News Events Student Affairs Business Affairs Employment Opportunities University Bookstore Technology Support Mining Minds Main Content <!--\n@media (min-width: 1250px) {\n    .topBanner {min-height:360px}\n}\n@media (max-width: 1249px) {\n    .topBanner {min-height:200px}\n}\n--> MINING MINDS \u201cMining Minds\u201d is an iconic piece of public art installed in 2010 to enhance The University of Texas at El Paso campus. At night, lights illuminate the steel structure, while LEDs emanate from the perforated ones and zeroes at each end of the pick. On special occasions, including historic dates, major annual milestones and to celebrate special accomplishments, the pick is illuminated in blue and orange. ICONIC PICK MARKS THE SPOT WHERE MINDS ARE MINED \u201cMining Minds,\u201d the creation of Denver-based artist/sculptor Michael Clapper, is a 25-foot-tall pickaxe head partly buried in a rock garden within the Sun Bowl-University roundabout. By itself, the impressive carbon steel artwork with the rusted finish resonates with the University\u2019s proud past, but there\u2019s more to this sculpture. Clapper added a perforated binary code \u2014 a pattern of ones and zeroes \u2014 on the top and bottom thirds of the pick to signify the language of modern technology. The artist, who prides himself on the amount of technology he puts into his projects, loosely based the code on an inspirational quote from UTEP President Emerita Diana Natalicio\u2019s 2008 Convocation speech: \u201cBelieve in yourself and in your dreams. Believe in UTEP and its aspirations. Share the dream!\u201d Special Occasions When the pick will be illuminated in blue and orange Minerpalooza December Graduation TCM Day May Graduation Homecoming Week Fall Convocation Honors Convocation Historic Dates Sept. 28: First day of the first class in 1914 April 16: Establishment of the State School of Mines and Metallurgy by the Texas Legislature in 1913 March 19: 1966 NCAA Basketball Championship Special Accomplishments Conference Championship National Championship Any other major national recognition approved by the President Special Accomplishments Conference Championship National Championship Any other major national recognition approved by the President <!--\n#important-dates .highligtsWrapper div:nth-child(8) img,\n#important-dates .highligtsWrapper div:nth-child(9) img {\n\tdisplay: none;\n}\n#important-dates .highligtsWrapper div:nth-child(9) {\n\tmargin-bottom: 30px;\n}\n--> #MiningMinds @utep_miners @utep_miners @eng_global_programs @adrngrcia @iamanouk__ @khaly10 Follow us instagram.com/@utep_miners <!--\n.carousel .carousel-control {\n    display:none;\n}\n@media(min-width: 1600px){\n\tbody .video-wrapper img,\n\tbody .video-wrapper video {\n\t\tmargin-top: -230px;\n\t}\n}\n@media(min-width: 1365px){\n\tbody .video-wrapper {\n\t\tmax-height: 535px !important;\n\t}\n\tbody .video-wrapper img,\n\tbody .video-wrapper video {\n\t\tmargin-top: -150px;\n\t}\n\n}\n\n@media(max-width: 991px){\n\t.highligtsWrapper .col-md-4 {\n\t\tmargin-left: auto;\n\t\tmargin-right: auto;\n\t}\n}\n\n.fw-section h2 {\n    text-transform: uppercase;\n    letter-spacing: 3px;\n}\n\n#orange-and-blue-image,\n#binary-code-image {\n\tmin-height: 600px;\n}\n#biography .container-narrow .row {\n    padding-top:20px;\n}\n\n#important-dates h2 {\n\tmargin-bottom: 60px;\n}\n#important-da