{"url": "https://www.utep.edu/research/otc/industry/licensing-process.html", "title": "Licensing Process", "text": "(function(d, s, id) {\n  var js, fjs = d.getElementsByTagName(s)[0];\n  if (d.getElementById(id)) return;\n  js = d.createElement(s); js.id = id;\n  js.src = \"//connect.facebook.net/en_US/sdk.js#xfbml=1&version=v2.7\";\n  fjs.parentNode.insertBefore(js, fjs);\n}(document, 'script', 'facebook-jssdk')); Skip to main content MinerAlert Search pages and people Toggle navigation Search Home Office of Technology Commercialization Mike Loya Center ---------------- Business Center Research Administration Research Compliance and Regulatory Assurances Research Advancement About About Us Frequently Asked Questions, Forms and Resources Inventors Submit Your Invention Patent Process Commercialization Process Start-Up Support Licensing Process Industry Available Technologies Licensing Process Search pages and people My UTEP Resources for INFORMATION FOR: Students Faculty & Staff Alumni Parents Quick Links X Quick Links Library Parking & Maps UTEP News Events Student Affairs Business Affairs Employment Opportunities University Bookstore Technology Support Office of Technology Commercialization Research & Innovation Main Content Licensing Process Contact Us Kelly Hall - 2nd Floor (915) 747-7820 techtransfer@utep.edu Staff Directory <!--\n.carousel-caption {\n    display: block !important;\n}\n--> Licensing For External Interest Are you a company interested in one of the patents available from the University of Texas at El Paso or interested in working with our faculty, contact us at techtransfer@utep.edu. We will set up a meeting to discuss licensing opportunities at the University. Licensing For Internal Interest Faculty and internal inventors, are you interested in getting your Intellectual Property out into the community. There are two routes available to you, first you can contact the Office of Tech Transfer and we can set up a meeting to discuss target fields to advertise too. The next route is if you are interested in creating your own start company. Creating your own start up can be a daunting task but, thankfully we have reasources available from the University that are at your disposal. We encourage you to reach out to the Office of Tech Transfer and the Mike Loya Center to get you started on your path to success. Licensing Overview MARKET THE INVENTION OTC uses the online database Flintbox to allow industry to search available UTEP technologies. We also attend conferences and industry events to promote our technology. Once interests are identified, we will work together with the inventors to describe the technical details and advantages of the invention to potential licensee under a Non-Disclosure Agreement (NDA) . DUE DILIGENCE Potential licensees are strongly suggested to conduct their own due diligence before entering into a licensing discussion with OTC. Here are some of the key questions: Evaluate the intellectual property proposed to be licensed and its potential value to your business. Can you develop the intellectual property independently without infringing? Is a patent likely to be issued? How strong is the patent? Will you need additional intellectual property protection around the patented technology and can you acquire it? OTC does not guarantee a patent will ever issue and it is up to the licensee to conduct its own due diligence before entering into a Patent License Agreement. OTC chooses a licensee based on a company\u2019s ability to commercialize the technology for the benefit of the general public. Generally, OTC requires a business plan or product development plan from the potential licensee. Sometimes an established company with experience in similar technologies and markets is the best choice for a particular technology. In other cases, the focus and intensity of a startup company is a better option. LICENSE THE INVENTION Here are the OTC sample Patent License Agreements. OTC has endorsed the Association of University Technology Manager\u2019s (AUTM) list of points to consider as companies license inventions from a university. AUTM\u2019s Points to Consider: Universities should reserve the right to practice licensed inventions and to allow other non-profit and governmental organizations to do so. Exclusive licenses should be structured in a manner that encourages technology development and use. Strive to minimize the licensing of \u201cfuture improvements\u201d Universities should anticipate and help to manage technology transfer related conflicts of interest. Ensure broad access to research tools. Be mindful of export regulations. Consider including provisions that address unmet needs, such as those of neglected patient populations or geographic areas, giving particular attention to improved therapeutics, diagnostics and agricultural technologies for the developing world. DEVELOP THE PRODUCT Licensees are welcome to continue the collaboration with UTEP by sponsoring research projects in the inventor\u2019s lab. If you have questions about sponsored research, please contact UTEP\u2019s Research & Innovation (R&I) . OTC works closely with ORSP on IP issues in Sponsored Research Agreements. UTEP generally retains ownership of patent rights and other intellectual property resulting from sponsored research. However, the sponsor may have rights to negotiate a license to the IP resulting from the research. The sponsor generally will not have contractual rights to discoveries that are outside of the scope of the research or that were invented prior to the research term. Therefore, it is important to define the scope of work within a research agreement and to review the IP provisions in the research contract. Many licensees require the active assistance of the inventors to facilitate their commercialization efforts. UTEP employee\u2019s participation with a startup or any consulting arrangement with a licensee is governed by UTEP conflict of interest policies. UTEP conflict of interest policies and online reporting portal can be found here . // <![CDATA[\n$(document).ready(function() { \n            \n                $(\"h2\").css(\"text-align\", \"left\"); \n                \n                $(\"a#bluelink\").css(\"color\", \"#337ab7\"); \n\n        \n        });\n// ]]> <!--\nbody {\n    position: relative;\n}\n\n/*header bg fix*/\n@media (min-width:1250px ) and (max-width: 1364px){\n\t.headerContainer {\n\t    width: 100%;\n\t}\n}\n/*Carousel FW*/\n#container + .container {\n\tmax-width: none !important;\n\twidth: auto!important;\n}\n.slide-transfer {\n    display:none!important;\n}\n.video-wrapper {\n\tmax-height: none !important;\n}\n.carousel-caption {\n\tdisplay: none;\n}\n.slide-transfer {\n\tdisplay: none;\n}\n/*FullWidthSection*/\n.fw-section {\n\tpadding-top: 40px;\n\tpadding-bottom: 60px;\n}\n.sectionAnchor {\n    padding-top:135px;\n    margin-top:-135px;\n}\n.fw-section h2 {\n\ttext-align: center;\n}\n.fw-section:after {\n\tclear: both;\n}\n.container-narrow {\n\tmax-width: 800px;\n}\n.row {\n\tmargin-left: 0;\n\tmargin-right: 0;\n}\n@media (max-width: 991px){\n    .col-md-4 {\n        width:100%;\n    }\n}\n/*Gray Bg Section*/\n.background-gray {\n\tbackground-color: #f2f2f2;\n}\n/*Blue Bg Section*/\n.background-blue {\n\tbackground-color: #041E42;\n\tcolor: #fff;\n}\n.background-blue h2 {\n\tcolor: #fff;\n}\n.background-blue .btn-primary, .background-blue a.btn-primary {\n    background-color: #FF8200;\n}\n.background-blue .btn-primary:hover, .background-blue .btn-primary:focus, .background-blue a.btn-primary:hover, .background-blue a.btn-primary:focus {\n    color: #041b3c;\n    background-color: #ddd;\n}\n/*flex*/\n.highligtsWrapper {\n    display: flex;\n    display: -webkit-flex;\n    flex-wrap: wrap;\n}\n.highligtsWrapper .well {\n\tbox-shadow:0px 2px 1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);-webkit-box-shadow:0px 2px 1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);;\n\theight: 100%;\n\tpadding: 0;\n}\n.highligtsBody {\n\tpadding: 0 24px 0 24px;