ional Consortium Convening Food Insecurity at UTEP Food insecurity is a reality for many college students, and UTEP is no exception. According to a 2022 study, over 60% of UTEP students identify as food insecure \u2014 meaning they lack reliable access to sufficient, affordable, and nutritious food. UTEP defines food insecurity as the limited availability of food, which may involve reduced quality, variety, or socially acceptable access to meals. To combat this, the Center for Community Engagement (CCE) sponsors a full-time Campus Compact AmeriCorps VISTA member who supports efforts to expand food access and build long-term, sustainable solutions. Through initiatives like Pete\u2019s Plate, the UTEP Food Pantry, and Mobile Pantry Mondays, the CCE has made measurable strides in addressing food insecurity: Over $20,000 of economic impact from food distributions More than 600 volunteer hours contributed by students and staff Supported weekly distributions providing over 600 meals per month at Pete\u2019s Plate during 2024-2025 Campus-wide outreach to promote sustainable food access solutions <!--\n#container div.main-content a.accHeading{\n        font-size: 18px;\n    }\n--> Meet Our AmeriCorps VISTA: Brianna Cooper Brianna Cooper serves as UTEP\u2019s 2024\u20132026 AmeriCorps VISTA for Food Security Initiatives through a partnership with Campus Compact . Based in the Center for Community Engagement, she supports efforts to combat campus hunger by expanding food access, building sustainable partnerships, and raising awareness of student food insecurity. Her work supports key initiatives like Pete\u2019s Plate , the UTEP Food Pantry , and Mobile Pantry Mondays , while also: Mapping and connecting stakeholders across campus and the community Maintaining partnerships such as with The Mustard Seed Caf\u00e9 Leading outreach efforts through events, tabling, and social media Supporting college-specific and faculty-led food access efforts Coordinating volunteer programs and data tracking \"I believe that no student should ever have to choose between focusing on school and having access to a nutritious meal. I\u2019m excited to be a part of building long-term solutions here at UTEP.\" \u2013 Brianna Cooper Mobile Pantry Volunteer Data-2024-2025 Download the Mobile Pantry 2024-2025 Volunteer Data On-Campus Food Resources UTEP Food Pantry Provides dry goods and non-perishable food items free of charge to currently enrolled students. \ud83d\udccd Visit the UTEP Food Pantry page Get Involved: Help Address Food Insecurity Volunteer to support weekly food distributions on campus. \ud83d\udd17 Sign up to volunteer Help Feed a Miner Support the Food Pantry through in-kind or financial donations. \ud83d\udcde Call (915) 747-8053 or email foodpantry@utep.edu to donate. \ud83d\udd17 Donate now Volunteer Leaderboard To celebrate and recognize the dedicated efforts of student volunteers, CCE is launching a Food Insecurity Volunteer Leader Board. The Leader Board will track volunteer hours contributed to: Mobile Pantry Mondays Food Pantry support and operations Outreach and engagement events Top volunteers will be highlighted on the website and recognized at an end-of-year celebration. Every hour makes a difference in ensuring no student goes hungry. Contact for Questions or to Collaborate cce-vista@utep.edu <!--\n    .accWrapper > div {\n    padding-left:24px;\n    }\n    .collapseToggleControl.collapsed span.glyphicon:before {\n        content: \"\\002b\";\n    }\n    .collapseToggleControl span.glyphicon:before {\n        content: \"\\2212\";\n    }\n    .optionalblock {\n        margin-top: 30px;\n        margin-bottom: 30px;\n    }\n /*flex*/\n.highligtsWrapper {\n    display: flex;\n    display: -webkit-flex;\n    flex-wrap: wrap;\n    margin-left: -14px;\n}\n.highligtsWrapper .col-md-4:nth-last-child(1) {\n    margin-bottom: 30px;\n}\n.highligtsWrapper .well {\n\tbox-shadow:0px 2px 1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);-webkit-box-shadow:0px 2px 1px rgba(0