{"url": "https://www.utep.edu/cce/community/community-partner-community-engagement.html", "title": "Community Engagement Courses (Service Learning)", "text": "(function(d, s, id) {\n  var js, fjs = d.getElementsByTagName(s)[0];\n  if (d.getElementById(id)) return;\n  js = d.createElement(s); js.id = id;\n  js.src = \"//connect.facebook.net/en_US/sdk.js#xfbml=1&version=v2.7\";\n  fjs.parentNode.insertBefore(js, fjs);\n}(document, 'script', 'facebook-jssdk')); Skip to main content MinerAlert Search pages and people Toggle navigation Search About About Us CCE Team Faculty Fellows Student Staff Careers Students Community Engagement Courses Community Work-Study Mentorship and Internship Program James M. Peak Scholarship Fund Faculty Community Engagement Courses Community Engaged Scholarship Partnering for Grant Funding Other Programs and Resources Community Connections Community Community Engagement Courses (Service Learning) Community Work-Study Mentorship and Internship Program Partnering for Grant Funding Community Partner Intake Form Request Volunteers Other Resources and Events Special Projects Southwest Regional Community Engagement Consortium Convening Food Insecurity Initiatives University  Impact Institutional Impact Provost\u2019s Community Engagement Council Carnegie Community Engagement Classification Resources Community Engagement and High Impact Practices in Higher Education Book Community of Engaged Scholars Office of the Provost and Division of Academic Affairs UTEP EDGE Civil Action Plan Handbook of Operating Procedures (Engaged Scholarship Policy 4.4.6.1.6) Search pages and people My UTEP Resources for INFORMATION FOR: Students Faculty & Staff Alumni Parents Quick Links X Quick Links Library Parking & Maps UTEP News Events Student Affairs Business Affairs Employment Opportunities University Bookstore Technology Support Center for Community Engagement Main Content UTEP Center for Community Engagement Community UTEP Center for Community Engagement Community Community Partnerships - Community Engagement Courses (Service Learning) About Join Us! The CUE CUE GivePulse Resource Videos Academic-Based Community Engagement Videos About Community-Engaged Coursework Community-Engaged Courses (CECs), also known as service-learning, are academic courses that integrate meaningful community engagement with classroom instruction and reflection. At UTEP, these courses are supported through the Center for Community Engagement (CCE) in partnership with local nonprofits, schools, government agencies, and other organizations committed to making a difference in the Paso del Norte region. The Role of Community Partners Community partners play a vital role in helping students connect academic learning with real-world experiences. By working with UTEP faculty and students, community organizations contribute to student growth, while also gaining support on projects and initiatives aligned with their mission. These academic partnerships provide students with hands-on opportunities to apply what they\u2019re learning in class to address real community challenges\u2014while building leadership skills, civic responsibility, and professional experience. In turn, community partners benefit from the energy, talent, and fresh perspective that students bring to their work. What Community Partners Can Expect As a community partner supporting CEC programming, you are viewed as a co-educator and mentor . Your insights, guidance, and opportunities enrich the student's learning experience and strengthen ties between the university and the broader community. Partners may support Community-Engaged Courses by: Participating in CCE-hosted training to understand expectations and best practices Maintaining regular communication with CCE staff and course faculty Mentoring and supervising students during their service Providing meaningful learning opportunities connected to course objectives Offering flexibility with student schedules, especially during midterms and finals Delivering a site orientation to introduce students to your organization and expectations Using The CUE platform Post or update service opportunities Track and verify student hours Manage your agency\u2019s account and profile Community Engagement Roles: <!--\nbody {\n      font-family: 'Open Sans', sans-serif;\n      background: #f8f9fa;\n      padding: 40px;\n    }\n\n    .tile-container {\n      display: flex;\n      flex-wrap: nowrap;\n      gap: 0;\n      -webkit-border-radius: 40px !important;\n      -moz-border-radius: 40px !important;\n      border-radius: 40px !important;\n      overflow: hidden;\n      max-width: 600px;\n      box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);\n      margin: 0 auto;\n    }\n\n    .tile {\n        flex: 1;\n        min-width: 0;\n        display: flex;\n        flex-direction: column;\n        align-items: center;\n        text-align: center;\n        padding: 30px 20px;\n        background: white;\n        color: #002855;\n        cursor: pointer;\n        transition: background 0.3s;\n        border-right: 1px solid #ddd;\n        text-decoration: none;\n        \n    }\n\n\n    .tile:last-child {\n      border-right: none;\n    }\n\n    .tile:hover {\n      background-color: #FF8200;\n    }\n\n    .tile img {\n      height: 100px;\n      width: 100px;\n      margin-bottom: 15px;\n      object-fit: contain;\n    }\n\n    .tile h3 {\n      font-size: 1.8rem;\n      color: #041E42;\n      margin: 0;\n      margin-bottom: 25px;\n      text-decoration: none;\n    }\n--> Faculty Homepage Students Join Us in Supporting Community-Engaged Courses Community-engaged courses at UTEP offer students the opportunity to apply classroom learning through hands-on service in partnership with local organizations. These service experiences are embedded into the curriculum and serve as a valuable component of students\u2019 academic and personal development. Students typically complete 10\u201320 hours of service through activities such as one-time events, semester-long projects, or agency visits. Faculty may collaborate with one or more community organizations\u2014either those they\u2019ve selected directly or those recommended by the Center for Community Engagement (CCE) . A portion of traditional coursework is replaced with a community-based activity that enhances student learning while supporting community needs. Below are three common models used in Community Engagement Courses: Models of Community-Engaged Coursework Model 1: Required Service Component (Minimum of 20 Hours) In this model, all students are required to complete a service-learning experience as part of the course. This expectation is outlined in the syllabus and tied directly to course objectives. Faculty may partner with one or more community organizations, allowing for deeper integration into the course through: Class discussions related to service work Writing assignments and reflections Site visits and guest speakers from partner organizations This model allows students to build interpersonal, teamwork, and organizational skills while working with diverse community members. Model 2: Project-Based Service This model involves collaborative, team-based work where students apply course concepts to a real-world community need. Projects may be: Designed by faculty in coordination with a partner agency, or Co-created by students and community members to address specific challenges Though student interaction with the agency may be more limited, this model emphasizes applied problem-solving and often requires specialized knowledge or research. Model 3: Community-Based Action Research This approach engages students in conducting participatory or action-oriented research in collaboration with a community partner. Students learn and apply research methodologies to: Explore issues of concern identified by the community Generate data and recommendations Share findings that support organizational planning or advocacy This model involves trust-building and long-term collaboration. It is ideal for agencies interested in research-informed action and willing to co-develop agendas with faculty. Often, these projects extend beyond a single semester. Semester Participation Timeline for Community Partners Community partners play a critical role in the success of CEC programming. A typical cycle of engagement includes: Complete the CEC Semester Application Attend Community Engagement Events hosted by the CCE Participate in Student Orientation or Training at the start of the semester Meet with Students to discuss roles, expectations, and organizational context Provide Mentorship and Supervision throughout the semester Verify Student Impact through The CUE or by signing timesheets Join End-of-Semester Reflections and Surveys to share feedback Ready to Get Involved? We welcome new and returning community partners interested in hosting UTEP students for semester-long or academic year engagement. The CCE team is here to support your participation and help align your organizational goals with student learning outcomes. To learn more or express interest, please contact us\u2014we\u2019re happy to connect you with the appropriate courses and faculty members. The CUE: Connecting Campus and Community The CUE is UTEP\u2019s centralized, web-based platform designed to connect students, faculty, and community partners with meaningful service and engagement opportunities across the El Paso region. Hosted on GivePulse , The CUE allows students to explore and sign up for volunteer experiences, internships, and community-based projects that align with their personal interests and academic goals. Students also use The CUE to find service opportunities that directly connect to their course requirements . Faculty members guide students on how to identify approved opportunities that align with course objectives, and students can link their CUE accounts to specific classes to track their engagement and meet academic expectations. What Community Partners Can Do on The CUE As a registered community partner on The CUE, your organization can: Post Volunteer Opportunities and Internships Recruit UTEP students for both short-term events and longer-term service experiences. Create and Manage Events Host and promote one-time or recurring events open to student volunteers. Track and Verify Student Impact (Hours) Review and approve student service hours and generate reports on engagement. Promote Events and Surveys Share donation campaigns, awareness events, and collect feedback through custom surveys. Increase Visibility Share your mission, programs, and needs with the UTEP community and connect with faculty and students who care about your cause. The CUE makes it easier than ever to build lasting partnerships between UTEP and the broader community\u2014supporting mutual learning, service, and impact. GivePulse for Adminstrators Basics of GivePulse (Administrator) A quick overview of basic GivePulse features for administrators GivePulse: Creating an event Learn how to create an event on GivePulse. GivePulse: User dashboard Learn about the User Dashboard on GivePulse. GivePulse: Manage Users Tutorial for how to manage users as an administrator on GivePulse. This video includes: how to access user information, changing user roles, and using invites as an onboarding device. GivePulse: Managing Impacts Learn how to manage impacts as an administrator on GivePulse. What is Community Engagement? Community Engagement\u00a0is a teaching method for students to gain academic content while also serving the community. Purpose of Community Engagement Courses? Community Engagement Courses serves the student, community, and the faculty member; it allows us to expand our university and to work alongside our community. How to Facilitate a Community Engagement\u00a0Consultant Model Community Engagement\u00a0consultant model is used in order to provide students an opportunity to apply their skills that they are building in statistical modeling and also work in service and with community organizations so that their expertise can have impact. Requirements for Classes If a faculty member is interested in participating or incorporating Community Engagement\u00a0in their class, we are here to help make that happen. Ethical Considerations of Community Engagement Courses Working in a Community Engagement Course\u00a0capacity is confidentiality and to protect the integrity of confidentiality of that agency. Co-Teaching with Community Partners Community Engagement Courses is not volunteerism, it has an academic component to it. Building Community Partnerships--What it Takes Partnerships is about starting where people are, finding ways of coming together to identify mutual interests not always needs, but mutual interest and it's a long-term relationship. What is True Reciprocity? Being engaged in Community Engagement Courses and learning about the history of a community, the culture, being active, developing relationships with people in the community is really at the heart of our profession. Preparing students for Community Engagement Courses In being able to help the students connect what we teach in the classroom, what we help them to learn, they can actually see in the community through real-life experiences. Effective Community Engagement\u00a0Reflection Reflection is really important, you don't separate your reflection from your observation, they go hand in hand. Identifying Appropriate Community Partners First step is finding a community organization whose focus area directly relates to the course content in the particular course that you want to offer Community Engagement Courses in. Community Engagement Courses with Freshmen Like most experiential learning, the student gains more by reflecting upon their experiences than simply by going through them. <!--\n    .accWrapper > div {\n    padding-left:24px;\n    }\n    .collapseToggleControl.collapsed span.glyphicon:before {\n        con