The Association of Fundraising Professionals West Texas \u2013 Southern New Mexico donated $5,000 to the University of Texas at El Paso in honor of James M. Peak, CFRE. They requested that $500 per semester be awarded until the gift is depleted. Eligible students must be pursuing a career related to non-profits. Graduate students are given preference but undergraduates are also considered. The must be full time students with a minimum GPA of 3.0. The recipient will be selected by a committee put together by the Director of the UTEP Center for Civic Engagement. Past Scholarship Recipients Sesar Macias Fall 2024 Scholarship Recipient Mariana Gutierrez Fall 2017 Scholarship Recipient Omar Altamira Fall 2017 Scholarship Recipient Nathalie Gonzalez Fall 2016 Scholarship Recipient Ricardo Garza Fall 2015 Scholarship Recipient Nadia Rivas Fall 2014 Scholarship Recipient Center for Community Engagement The CUE Website Civic Engagement Programs Give Now Connect With Us The University of Texas at El Paso Center for Community Engagement Physical Address: 1610 Hawthorne El Paso, Texas 79902 E: cce@utep.edu P: (915) 747-7969 THE UNIVERSITY OF TEXAS AT EL PASO CARES Act Compliance Clery Crime Statistics Emergency Information Employment Mental Health Resources Public Course Information Report Fraud Required Links State Reports Texas Veterans Portal Title IX Reporting Title IX Sexual Misconduct Policy UT System Web Accessibility Web Privacy Policy 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968 | 915-747-5000 | SITE FEEDBACK Modal X"}
{"url": "https://expertise.utep.edu/profiles/vladik", "title": "Vladik Kreinovich | Expertise Connector", "text": "Enhancements HELP PEOPLE CENTERS COMMUNITIES RESEARCH STORIES .wrap-main-search{\n    position: relative;\n    left: 10px;\n    bottom: 17px;\n  }\n  .search-main-box {\n   display: flex;\n  }\n  .searchTerm-main-box {\n    width: 100%;\n    border: 1px solid #b1b3b3;\n    border-right: none;\n    padding: 5px;\n    background: #ffffff;\n    outline: none;\n    color: #041E42;\n  }\n  .searchTerm-main-box:focus{\n    color: #041E42;\n  }\n  .searchButton-main-box {\n    width: 40px;\n    border: 1px solid #041E42;\n    background: #041E42;\n    text-align: center;\n    color: #fff;\n    cursor: pointer;\n    font-size: 20px;\n  } Vladik Kreinovich 3.0404 Chemistry and Computer Science Building El Paso Texas , 79968 vladik@utep.edu (915) 747-6951 Web Site Community Cyber-Security NETWORKS .networkprofile\n{\ntext-decoration: none;\n} Vladik Y Kreinovich Professor, Computer Science - Cyber-Share - Bioinformatics - Computational Science .titleEventStyle{\n  font-size: 12px;\n  font-weight: bolder;\n  position: relative;\n  font-family: \"Myriad Pro\", \"Trebuchet MS\", \"Arial\", sans-serif;\n} Dr. Kreinovich's main area of expertise is dealing with uncertainty and imprecision. There are two main aspects to uncertainty and imprecision. The first aspect is that data comes from measurements, and measurements are never absolutely accurate. It is important to analyze how this uncertainty affects our predictions, and how to make decisions under such uncertainty. Traditional statistical techniques assume that we know the probabilities of different measurement inaccuracies, but in many cases, we only know the upper bound on the measurement error. For example, if the measured value is 1 V and we know that the measurement error does not exceed 0.1 V, then the only information that we have about the actual voltage is that it is somewhere in the interval [0.1, 1.1]. Different values from these intervals may lead to different results of data processing. It is desirable to find the range of possible values. Interval computations also help when we know bounds on probabilities. The second aspect is that experts often describe their knowledge not in terms of precise rules, but by using imprecise words from natural language. It is then necessary to describe this knowledge in precise computer-understandable terms in order to process this knowledge. This area of research is extremely important to interdisciplinary research, where we need to combine different research areas with different levels of rigor. The main goal of all our algorithms is to process data, and the objectives are set up by the corresponding specialists. In processing such data, Dr. Kreinovich has collaborated with specialists in radio astronomy, geoscience, environmental science, and other areas of biology. .profile-titleStatment\n{\ncolor:#FF8200;\nfont-size:20px;\nfont-family: \"trebuchet ms\";\ntext-transform:uppercase;\n}\n.profile-infoStatement\n{\n\nfont-family: Verdana,Geneva,sans-serif;\nfont-size: 11px;\n}\n.profile-link\n{\ncolor:#FF8200 !important;\n}\n\n.awd_profile_link{\nposition: absolute;\nleft: 624px;\ntop: 0px;\ntext-decoration: underline;\n}\n\n.patent_profile_link{\nposition: absolute;\nleft: 624px;\ntop: 16px;\ntext-decoration: underline;\n}\n\n.student_profile_link{\nposition: absolute;\nleft: 624px;\ntop: 31px;\ntext-decoration: underline;\n} KEYWORDS Uncertainty Interval Computations Decision Making Under Uncertainty Use of Symmetries .expertise_keywords_custom_container{\n/*position: relative;\ntop: 14px;\nleft: 12px;*/\n}\n\n.in_the_new_title{\ncolor: #e47d00;\nfont-weight: bold;\nfont-size: 18px;\nfont-family: \"Trebuchet MS\";\n}\n\n.list_keywords{\nline-height: 14px;\n/*position: relative;\ntop: 13px;*/\n}\n\n\n#toggle_keywords{\nposition: relative;\nleft: 170px;\nbottom: 12px;\ncursor: pointer;\nfont-family: \"Verdana, Geneva, Sans-Serif