{"url": "https://expertise.utep.edu/profiles/jamunoz", "title": "Jorge Munoz | Expertise Connector", "text": "Enhancements HELP PEOPLE CENTERS COMMUNITIES RESEARCH STORIES .wrap-main-search{\n    position: relative;\n    left: 10px;\n    bottom: 17px;\n  }\n  .search-main-box {\n   display: flex;\n  }\n  .searchTerm-main-box {\n    width: 100%;\n    border: 1px solid #b1b3b3;\n    border-right: none;\n    padding: 5px;\n    background: #ffffff;\n    outline: none;\n    color: #041E42;\n  }\n  .searchTerm-main-box:focus{\n    color: #041E42;\n  }\n  .searchButton-main-box {\n    width: 40px;\n    border: 1px solid #041E42;\n    background: #041E42;\n    text-align: center;\n    color: #fff;\n    cursor: pointer;\n    font-size: 20px;\n  } Jorge Munoz 312C Physical Sciences Building El Paso Texas , 79968 jamunoz@utep.edu (915) 747-7541 Web Site Community Interdisciplinary Research and Education (IDRE) Undergraduate Research Neuroscience Entrepreneurship Community of Practice Planetary Science and Astrobiology NETWORKS .networkprofile\n{\ntext-decoration: none;\n} Jorge Alberto Munoz Associate Professor, Physics .titleEventStyle{\n  font-size: 12px;\n  font-weight: bolder;\n  position: relative;\n  font-family: \"Myriad Pro\", \"Trebuchet MS\", \"Arial\", sans-serif;\n} Phase stability is one of the most fundamental phenomena in the universe, and prediction of structure and properties of materials using computers is nowadays one of the most important problems in applied science. Dr. Jorge Mu\u00f1oz and his group study the quantum mechanical origins of the phase stability of materials by developing computational, data science, and machine learning methods and tools to extend the reach of first-principles atomistic simulations of materials. Changes induced by pressure, temperature, chemical ordering, etc. in the electronic structure and magnetism of elemental metals and metallic alloys and their effect on the phonons and phonon entropy are of particular interest. The group also develops machine learning pipelines and data science solutions for applied problems in physics. .profile-titleStatment\n{\ncolor:#FF8200;\nfont-size:20px;\nfont-family: \"trebuchet ms\";\ntext-transform:uppercase;\n}\n.profile-infoStatement\n{\n\nfont-family: Verdana,Geneva,sans-serif;\nfont-size: 11px;\n}\n.profile-link\n{\ncolor:#FF8200 !important;\n}\n\n.awd_profile_link{\nposition: absolute;\nleft: 624px;\ntop: 0px;\ntext-decoration: underline;\n}\n\n.patent_profile_link{\nposition: absolute;\nleft: 624px;\ntop: 16px;\ntext-decoration: underline;\n}\n\n.student_profile_link{\nposition: absolute;\nleft: 624px;\ntop: 31px;\ntext-decoration: underline;\n} KEYWORDS Computational physics Phase stability and thermodynamics Machine learning Data science Atomistic simulations of materials Density Functional Theory .expertise_keywords_custom_container{\n/*position: relative;\ntop: 14px;\nleft: 12px;*/\n}\n\n.in_the_new_title{\ncolor: #e47d00;\nfont-weight: bold;\nfont-size: 18px;\nfont-family: \"Trebuchet MS\";\n}\n\n.list_keywords{\nline-height: 14px;\n/*position: relative;\ntop: 13px;*/\n}\n\n\n#toggle_keywords{\nposition: relative;\nleft: 170px;\nbottom: 12px;\ncursor: pointer;\nfont-family: \"Verdana, Geneva, Sans-Serif\";\nfont-size: 12px;\ncolor: #13275d;\ntext-decoration: underline;\n} $(document).ready(function () {\n\n    var max = 5\n    if ($(this).find('li').length > max) {\n       \n        $(\"#list_keywords li:gt(\" + max + \")\").hide()   \n        $(\".toggle_keywords\").click(function() {\n           $(\"#list_keywords li:gt(\" + max + \")\").toggle();\n           label = $(this);\n            if(label.html()==\"View more\"){\n                label.html('View less');\n            }else{\n                label.html('View more');\n            }\n        });             \n    }\n}); CUSTOMIZED LINKS Featured awards ProQuest/Pivot funding opportunities .in_the_new_title{\n    color: #e47d00;\n    font-weight: bold;\n    font-size: 18px;\n    font-f