{"url": "https://www.utep.edu/audit/general-info/faq.html", "title": "Frequently Asked Questions", "text": "(function(d, s, id) {\n  var js, fjs = d.getElementsByTagName(s)[0];\n  if (d.getElementById(id)) return;\n  js = d.createElement(s); js.id = id;\n  js.src = \"//connect.facebook.net/en_US/sdk.js#xfbml=1&version=v2.7\";\n  fjs.parentNode.insertBefore(js, fjs);\n}(document, 'script', 'facebook-jssdk')); Skip to main content MinerAlert Search pages and people Toggle navigation Search About About Us Mission & Vision Statement Internship Program General Information Services Provided Internal Audit Committee Professional Standards Professional Organizations and Certifications Resources Frequently Asked Questions People Meet Our Staff Organization Chart Search pages and people My UTEP Resources for INFORMATION FOR: Students Faculty & Staff Alumni Parents Quick Links X Quick Links Library Parking & Maps UTEP News Events Student Affairs Business Affairs Employment Opportunities University Bookstore Technology Support Office of Auditing and Consulting Services Main Content UTEP Office of Auditing and Consulting Services General Information UTEP Office of Auditing and Consulting Services General Information Frequently Asked Questions What is the function of the Internal Audit Department? The Internal Audit Department performs reviews to help determine if appropriate controls exist over administrative processes and/or systems. We offer consulting services that can improve the efficiency and effectiveness of your administrative processes. What should I expect when an audit is scheduled for my department? Generally, the Department Head or Chair will be notified of an audit\u00a0of their area through the issuance of an engagement letter.\u00a0 Subsequently, the assigned auditor will contact the Department Head/Chair and schedule a meeting to discuss the scope of the audit and logistics of conducting the audit.\u00a0 At this meeting, department personnel are encouraged to discuss concerns or ask questions about the audit.\u00a0 A typical audit has the following stages: Planning The assigned auditor will review files of prior audits in your area, review departmental literature, research applicable policies, and develop an audit program which lists the steps in which the audit will be performed. Entrance Meeting This meeting introduces department personnel to the auditor in charge and allows personnel to share concerns and ask questions. Fieldwork The auditor performs tests of paper and electronic documents. Interviews of department staff will also be conducted. Throughout the audit, the auditor will inform the Department Head/Chair of possible discrepancies. Report Writing The auditor will write a report which states audit procedures, audit results and recommendations for improvement. After the report is completed, a draft will be given to Department head/Chair for review. All reports are formally issued to the University President. Exit Meeting The exit meeting is a meeting between the Department Head/Chair and Audit staff. A review of the report and findings will be discussed along with suggested recommendations. Occasionally, oral recommendations are made on items not included in the report. How long will an audit take? Audits can last from several days to several months, depending on the complexity of the audit. How will the findings be reported? You will be updated throughout the course of the audit so that there are no surprises. At the conclusion of the audit, you will be able to review a draft of the report before the final report is issued. Final reports are issued to the University president with copies distributed to the Department Head/Chair, the respective Vice President, and U.T. System Administration. Are there different types of audits? There are four types of audits performed: Financial Audits These audits address questions of accounting, recording, and reporting of financial transactions. Compliance Audits These audits seek to determine if departments are adhering to State, Federal, and U.T. System rules, policies, and procedures. Information Technology Audits These audit address the internal control environment of automated information processing systems and how these systems are used. These audits typically evaluate system input, output and processing controls, backup and recovery plans, and system security, as well as computer facility reviews. Departmental Audits These audits review the adequacy of internal controls within the department and determines whether a control conscious environment exists. What should I do if I suspect fraud? If you suspect fraud or non-compliance in your workplace, report it immediately by contacting our office or the UTEP Compliance Office. You will not be required to identify yourself. Office of Auditing and Consulting Services Administration Bldg., Suite 402 Telephone #: (915) 747-5191 FAX #: (915) 747-8913 E-mail: audit@utep.edu Office of Institutional Compliance Kelly Hall, Room 308 Telephone #: (888) 228-7713 E-mail: compliance@utep.edu You may also report anonymously online at: www.reportlineweb.com/utep . <!--\n.accWrapper div {\npadding-left:36px;\n}\n.collapseToggleControl.collapsed span.glyphicon:before {\n    content: \"\\002b\";\n}\n.collapseToggleControl span.glyphicon:before {\n    content: \"\\2212\";\n}\n--> General Information Services Provided Internal Audit Committee Professional Standards Professional Organizations and Certifications Resources Frequently Asked Questions Resources Frequently Asked Questions Connect With Us The University of Texas at El Paso Office of Auditing and Consulting Services Administration Building,  Suite 402 500 West University Avenue El Paso, Texas 79968 E: audit@utep.edu P: (915) 747-5191 F: (915) 747-8913 THE UNIVERSITY OF TEXAS AT EL PASO CARES Act Compliance Clery Crime Statistics Emergency Information Employment Mental Health Resources Public Course Information Report Fraud Required Links State Reports Texas Veterans Portal Title IX Reporting Title IX Sexual Misconduct Policy UT System Web Accessibility Web Privacy Policy 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968 | 915-747-5000 | SITE FEEDBACK Modal X"}
{"url": "https://www.utep.edu/financial-aid/eligibility/satisfactory-academic-progress.html", "title": "Satisfactory Academic Progress", "text": "(function(d, s, id) {\n  var js, fjs = d.getElementsByTagName(s)[0];\n  if (d.getElementById(id)) return;\n  js = d.createElement(s); js.id = id;\n  js.src = \"//connect.facebook.net/en_US/sdk.js#xfbml=1&version=v2.7\";\n  fjs.parentNode.insertBefore(js, fjs);\n}(document, 'script', 'facebook-jssdk')); Skip to main content MinerAlert Search pages and people Toggle navigation Search How to Apply Applying for Financial Aid Scholarships Types of Aid The Paydirt Promise The Miner Success Grant Scholarships Financial Aid Grants Student Employment Tuition Waivers and Exemptions Student Loans Summer Financial Aid Financial Aid by College Financial Aid for Transfer Students Financial Aid for Military Students Financial Aid for Graduate Students Eligibility Who is Eligible for Financial Aid How Are My Awards Calculated How Do I Stay Eligible More Information Your Cost of Attendance For Parents Other Ways to Pay for College Our Policies Code of Conduct Consumer Information Resources Guide to Goldmine Forms Upload Your Documents Sign Up for Direct Deposit Net Price Calculator Frequently Asked Questions Contact Us Contact Us Leadership Search pages and people My UTEP Resources for INFORMATION FOR: Students Faculty & Staff Alumni Parents Quick Links X Quick Links Library Parking & Maps UTEP News Events Student Affairs Business Affairs Employment Opportunities University Bookstore Technology Support Financial Aid and Scholarships Main Content UTEP Financial Aid and Scholarships Eligibility UTEP Financial Aid and Scholarships Eligibility Satisfactory Academic Progress (SAP) To remain eligible for financial aid , you must make satisfactory academic progress . SAP requirements apply to all coursework, including transferred credits, even if you did not receive financial aid at any point. The general requirements are: Minimum GPA: 2.0 or higher for undergraduate students. 2.5 for certain programs, like pharmacy. 3.0 or higher for graduate students. Completion rate: pass 67% or more of all attempted credits. Maximum timeframe: you must be on track to complete your program without attempting more than 150% of the total hours on your degree plan. For example, if your program requires 120 credits, you must be able to graduate without exceeding 180 attempted credits (150% of 120 is 180). We review your SAP status at the end of each semester, after final grades are posted, and we will notify you if you lose or are at risk of losing your eligibility for financial aid. You can see your SAP status in the student portal, Goldmine. Here is a description of each possible status: Description of each SAP status Eligible for Aid You are meeting all SAP requirements and can receive financial aid. You should continue to do well in your classes to make sure you remain eligible for financial aid. Warning Semester You failed to meet either the GPA and/or completion rate SAP requirements. You continue to be eligible for financial aid for one semester, but must return to good standing by the end of the warning semester to avoid losing your financial aid eligibility in the future. Not Eligible for Aid You failed to meet either the GPA and/or completion rate SAP requirements after a warning semester, and, unfortunately, you are no longer eligible for financial aid at this time. However, if you have an extenuating circumstance, you may appeal. Complete a Satisfactory Academic Progress (SAP) Appeal Form , and upload it along with required documentation to financialaid.utep.edu/upload , no later than the beginning of finals week to determine your eligibility for the next term. Note: You can also regain your financial aid eligibility by continuing to attend classes without using financial aid until you meet SAP requirements again. Terminated Regrettably, you are\u00a0no longer\u00a0meeting SAP because you have exceeded or will exceed the\u00a0maximum number of credits to complete your degree or you have exhausted the maximum number of appeals allowed. In order to continue to fund your education, you may look for alternative sources of funding. Probation/Appeal Approved Your SAP appeal has been approved and your financial aid eligibility has been reinstated, however, you must consistently meet the conditions of your academic plan to continue to receive financial aid. Appeal Denied Your SAP appeal has been denied and you are not eligible for financial aid this semester. Note: You can also regain your financial aid eligibility by continuing to attend classes without using financial aid until you meet SAP requirements again. <!--\n    .accWrapper > div {\n    padding-left:24px;\n    }\n    .collapseToggleControl.collapsed span.glyphicon:before {\n        content: \"\\002b\";\n    }\n    .collapseToggleControl span.glyphicon:before {\n        content: \"\\2212\";\n    }\n    .optionalblock {\n        margin-top: 30px;\n        margin-bottom: 30px;\n    }\n /*flex*/\n.highligtsWrapper {\n    display: flex;\n    display: -webkit-flex;\n    flex-wrap: wrap;\n    margin-left: -14px;\n}\n.highligtsWrapper .col-md-4:nth-last-child(1) {\n    margin-bottom: 30px;\n}\n.highligtsWrapper .well {\n\tbox-shadow:0px 2px 1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);-webkit-box-shadow:0px 2px 1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);;\n\theight: 100%;\n\tpadding: 0;\n}\n.highligtsBody {\n\tpadding: 0 24px 0 24px;\n}\n.highligtsBody .btn-primary {\n    \tdisplay:block;\n    \tmargin-top: 30px;\n}\n\n.highligtsWrapper .col-md-4 {\n   margin-bottom: 30px;\n}\n\n.lightWhiteBg {\n\tbackground: rgba(255,255,255,.85);\n    padding: 4px 24px 24px 24px;\n    margin-bottom: 20px;\n}\n--> <!--\ndiv.facebook .pp_top .pp_left{background:#fff;}div.facebook .pp_top .pp_middle{background:#fff;}div.facebook .pp_top .pp_right{background:#fff;}div.facebook .pp_content_container .pp_left{background:#fff;}div.facebook .pp_content_container .pp_right{background:#fff;}di