ts have been developed to help make these processes more efficient. The Information Security Office is ready to assist you in this endeavor. As a Systems Administrator, You Will be Required to Comply/Perform the Following Functions: Install patches Review system logs Report malicious or suspicious activity on systems to ISO immediately Report sensitive information stored on systems to ISO Maintain user access administration Disaster recovery planning Physical security Disable unnecessary services on servers Generate/Retain system backups Identify / designate a secondary system administrator(s) Comply with password requirements Access control Environmental protection (i.e., protection from possible exposure to water damage, excessive heat, etc.) 3 Hours of Cyber Security Training is required every fiscal year as mandated by the Texas Cybersecurity Act for Higher Education employees (Information Resources Employees-IREs) System audit logging Maintain minimum security standards for systems Monitoring of system activity Note: Please note that the list above is neither complete nor comprehensive. For all policies that must be adhered to please refer to the resources listed on this page. Other Resources UTEP Minimum Security Standards for Systems (PDF) Standard 9: Data Classification Standard (PDF) Extended List of Confidential Data (PDF) Protection of Digital Research The University of Texas at El Paso Security Policies UTEP Minimum Security Standards for Data Stewardship UTEP Change Management Guidelines The Handbook of Operating Procedures (HOOP) Federal Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA) UTS165 Information Resources Use and Security Policy Texas Administrative Code 202 (TAC 202) Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI DSS) Information Security Awareness for Systems Administrators - MS PowerPoint Presentation Security Awareness Security Awareness How Do I Protect Myself General Security Tips Protecting Yourself From Identity Theft Best Password Practi