{"url": "https://www.utep.edu/information-resources/iso/security-awareness/technical-security-resources/what-is-html-injection.html", "title": "HTML Injection", "text": "(function(d, s, id) {\n  var js, fjs = d.getElementsByTagName(s)[0];\n  if (d.getElementById(id)) return;\n  js = d.createElement(s); js.id = id;\n  js.src = \"//connect.facebook.net/en_US/sdk.js#xfbml=1&version=v2.7\";\n  fjs.parentNode.insertBefore(js, fjs);\n}(document, 'script', 'facebook-jssdk')); Skip to main content MinerAlert Search pages and people Toggle navigation Search About About Services Services Security Awareness Email Encryption Frequently Asked Questions Resources Resources File Sharing Troubleshooting Password Related Issues Bitdefender Information Duo Two-Factor Authentication Change of Address FAQ Laptop/Desktop Encryption SSN Solicitation and Use Handbook of Operating Procedures Required Computer Software Policies Policies Payment Card Industry Web Privacy Policies Search pages and people My UTEP Resources for INFORMATION FOR: Students Faculty & Staff Alumni Parents Quick Links X Quick Links Library Parking & Maps UTEP News Events Student Affairs Business Affairs Employment Opportunities University Bookstore Technology Support Information Security Office Information Resources Main Content UTEP Information Resources Information Security Office Security Awareness Technical Security Resources UTEP Information Resources Information Security Office Security Awareness Technical Security Resources What is HTML Injection HTML Injection also known as Cross Site Scripting. It is a security vulnerability that allows an attacker to inject HTML code into web pages that are viewed by other users. Attackers often inject malicious JavaScript, VBScript, ActiveX,\u00a0and/or\u00a0HTML into vulnerable applications to deceive the user in order to gather data from them.\u00a0 Cross-site scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities can be used by attackers to bypass authentication controls there by gaining access to sensitive data on your system. Well crafted malicious code can even help the attacker gain access to the entire system. Example Of Html Injection First the attacker finds out a site which is vulnerable to HTML injection Then the attacker sends the URL with malicious code injected in the URL to the victim user either through email or\u00a0some other\u00a0mechanism. If the victim user click this malicious URL, it will run the JavaScript or VBScript code with the privileges of the victim user. Depending on the code being executed it can give out sensitive information of the user or even compromise the victim\u2019s computer. Avoiding Html Injections? Web programming best practices should include: Validation of user input by checking for length, type, format and data range. Encode any user input that will be output by the application. Please check the following links for more information. Tool to check .NET code for XSS vulnerabilities https://www.owasp.org/index.php/Reviewing_code_for_XSS_issues#Protecting_against_XSS http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cross-site_scripting https://www.cgisecurity.com/articles/xss-faq.html Security Awareness Security Awareness How Do I Protect Myself General Security Tips Protecting Yourself From Identity Theft Best Password Practices Protecting Yourself From Phishing Securing Your Laptop How to Release-Add Sender to Safelist.pdf Email Encryption Services - CRES CyberAttacks.pdf Technical Security Resources Denial of Service Attack SQL Injection HTML Injection How Do I Protect Confidential Information Receive Confidential Information? System Administrator Responsibilities What Are We Doing To Protect You? What is UTEP Doing About SPAM? What is UTEP Doing About Viruses? Training Programs On Security Awareness Change of Address FAQ Statistics Spam Statistics Copyright Infringements Peer-To-Peer File Sharing Required Computer Software Service Catalog Helpdesk Connect With Us The University of Texas at El Paso Information Security Office In