{"url": "https://www.utep.edu/information-resources/iso/security-awareness/technical-security-resources/what-is-sql-injection.html", "title": "SQL Injection", "text": "(function(d, s, id) {\n  var js, fjs = d.getElementsByTagName(s)[0];\n  if (d.getElementById(id)) return;\n  js = d.createElement(s); js.id = id;\n  js.src = \"//connect.facebook.net/en_US/sdk.js#xfbml=1&version=v2.7\";\n  fjs.parentNode.insertBefore(js, fjs);\n}(document, 'script', 'facebook-jssdk')); Skip to main content MinerAlert Search pages and people Toggle navigation Search About About Services Services Security Awareness Email Encryption Frequently Asked Questions Resources Resources File Sharing Troubleshooting Password Related Issues Bitdefender Information Duo Two-Factor Authentication Change of Address FAQ Laptop/Desktop Encryption SSN Solicitation and Use Handbook of Operating Procedures Required Computer Software Policies Policies Payment Card Industry Web Privacy Policies Search pages and people My UTEP Resources for INFORMATION FOR: Students Faculty & Staff Alumni Parents Quick Links X Quick Links Library Parking & Maps UTEP News Events Student Affairs Business Affairs Employment Opportunities University Bookstore Technology Support Information Security Office Information Resources Main Content UTEP Information Resources Information Security Office Security Awareness Technical Security Resources UTEP Information Resources Information Security Office Security Awareness Technical Security Resources What is SQL Injection When a web application fails to sanitize user supplied input entered in the input fields, it is possible to enter malicious SQL statements, thereby providing the attacker the ability to retrieve more data from databases. The impact of the attack can allow attackers to gain total control of the database or even the system. Detecting Sql Injections First step is to inventory your web pages which accept user input and connect to a backend\u00a0database server for data access. Second step is to\u00a0identify all input fields which accept \u00a0values that can be used in crafting SQL queries. The third step is to test application for SQL injections using the following procedures: Enter a semicolon (;) or single quote (\u2018) to the input field. If the input field is not being filtered correctly , it will generate an error message which tells you that it is vulnerable to SQL injection. Also comments (--) and other SQL keywords like OR and AND can be used to test the input field. Avoiding Sql Injections Web programming best practices should include: Validate user input by adding checking for\u00a0variable Length, Type, Format and Range. When possible reject input values that contain semi-colon, single quote, comment delimiter (--), comment delimiter (/*\u2026*/) and (xp_) Please refer to the following links for more detailed information on SQL injections http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SQL_injection http://www.owasp.org/index.php/SQL_Injection http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/ms161953.aspx http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/ms994921.aspx Security Awareness Security Awareness How Do I Protect Myself General Security Tips Protecting Yourself From Identity Theft Best Password Practices Protecting Yourself From Phishing Securing Your Laptop How to Release-Add Sender to Safelist.pdf Email Encryption Services - CRES CyberAttacks.pdf Technical Security Resources Denial of Service Attack SQL Injection HTML Injection How Do I Protect Confidential Information Receive Confidential Information? System Administrator Responsibilities What Are We Doing To Protect You? What is UTEP Doing About SPAM? What is UTEP Doing About Viruses? Training Programs On Security Awareness Change of Address FAQ Statistics Spam Statistics Copyright Infringements Peer-To-Peer File Sharing Required Computer Software Service Catalog Helpdesk Connect With Us The University of Texas at El Paso Information Security Office Information Security Office 500 W University El