ce of the Provost and Vice President for Academic Affairs Main Content UTEP Office of the Provost and Vice President for Academic Affairs Policies UTEP Office of the Provost and Vice President for Academic Affairs Policies Occupational License Eligibility The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) offers programs that may prepare an individual for an initial occupational license as defined under Texas Occupations Code 58.001. In accordance with state law, please be advised of the following: An individual who has been convicted of an offense may be ineligible for issuance of an occupational license upon completion of the educational program; Local or county licensing authorities may issue additional guidelines related to criminal history. Applicants should contact their respective local or county licensing authority for more details. A person may request a criminal history evaluation letter regarding the personal eligibility for a license from the licensing authority under Texas Occupations Code 53.102. Licensing authorities that issue occupational licenses to individuals who complete an educational program, must establish guidelines which state the reasons a particular crime is considered to relate to a particular license, as well as any other criterion that affects the decisions of the licensing authority. Guidelines related to issuance of an occupational license for educational programs offered at UTEP can be found in the table below. Students are encouraged to review all applicable eligibility requirements related to the respective occupational license. Questions related to eligibility requirements should be directed to the applicable licensing authority. Occupational License Helpful Links College of Education College of Health Sciences College of Liberal Arts College of Science College of Nursing School of Pharmacy Major, Degree Credential External Agency External Link BS in Education Teacher Certification Texas Education Agency https://tea.texas.gov/Texas_Educat