{"url": "https://www.utep.edu/programs/graduate/electrical-and-computer-engineering-phd.html", "title": "Electrical and Computer Engineering Ph.D.", "text": "College of Engineering Electrical and Computer Engineering, Ph.D. The research-oriented Ph.D. in Electrical and Computer Engineering gives students deep knowledge in a subfield of the discipline. They participate in the discovery and generation of new knowledge and technology through research and engage in local, national and international collaborations that provide opportunities for further research and technical training, and developing professional networks. .program-overview-section .w-col-5 {\n\tdisplay: none;\n}\n.program-overview-section .w-col.w-col-7 {\n    margin: 0 auto;\n    width: 960px;\n}\n@media screen and (max-width: 991px) {\n\t.program-overview-section .w-col.w-col-7 {\n\t    width: 100%;\n\t}\n}\n.pp-wrapper {\n    background-position: 50% 0;\n} Request info Work on collaborative research projects with industry, national labs and other universities Conduct research in state-of-the-art labs, including a 6,000-square-foot nanofabrication facility Fellowships, scholarships and graduate assistantships for research and teaching are available Research Training Leads to Leadership Position at Blue Origin It wasn’t just the classes that prepared alumnus Rodolfo Aguirre for an engineering career with aerospace company Blue Origin. The research opportunities and supportive professors in UTEP’s doctoral program in electrical and computer engineering taught him how to spot gaps in research and develop new ideas — skills he uses to find novel solutions to challenges that may arise when sending rockets to the moon. Rodolfo Aguirre Ph.D. in Electrical and Computer Engineering, 2017 Career Opportunities Graduates of the Electrical and Computer Engineering Ph.D. are prepared for careers in industry, government research laboratories, academia or to start their own companies. Recent doctoral graduates have be