{"url": "https://www.utep.edu/business/graduate/msecon/msecon.html", "title": "Master of Science in Economics with Data Analytics", "text": "(function(d, s, id) {\n  var js, fjs = d.getElementsByTagName(s)[0];\n  if (d.getElementById(id)) return;\n  js = d.createElement(s); js.id = id;\n  js.src = \"//connect.facebook.net/en_US/sdk.js#xfbml=1&version=v2.7\";\n  fjs.parentNode.insertBefore(js, fjs);\n}(document, 'script', 'facebook-jssdk')); Skip to main content MinerAlert Search pages and people Toggle navigation Search About About Hunt College Accreditation Contact Us Our Mission and Vision Hunt Insights Hunt Connect Academic Programs Undergraduate Programs Master of Accountancy Master of Business Administration (MBA) Master of Science in Economics with Data Analytics Ph.D. in Business Administration Certificate of Accountancy Certificate in Innovation and Entrepreneurship Corporate Academies at Hunt College Departments Accounting and Information Systems Department Economics and Finance Marketing, Management and Supply Chain People Current Doctoral Students Dean's Office Faculty UTEP Campus Directory Research Center for Free Enterprise Border Region Modeling Project Center for Multicultural Management and Ethics Hunt Institute The Mike Loya Center for Innovation and Commerce Seminars Alumni and Partners Alumni Business Advisory Council Giving to UTEP Distinguished Alumni Gold Nuggets Resources Student Resources Faculty and Staff Resources BUSN Space Reservation Search pages and people My UTEP Resources for INFORMATION FOR: Students Faculty & Staff Alumni Parents Quick Links X Quick Links Library Parking & Maps UTEP News Events Student Affairs Business Affairs Employment Opportunities University Bookstore Technology Support Woody L. Hunt College of Business Main Content UTEP Woody L. Hunt College of Business graduate msecon UTEP Woody L. Hunt College of Business graduate msecon MASTER OF SCIENCE IN ECONOMICS WITH DATA ANALYTICS The Master of Science in Economics with Data Analytics provides students with a theoretical foundation in economic concepts, as well as hands-on experience with large datasets and statistical software packages, including STATA, R, and Python, used in the public and private sectors. Upon completion of the 30-hour program, students will earn a Master of Science in Economics and a Graduate Certificate in Big Data Analytics. Courses below marked with a * are counted as part of the Graduate Certificate in Big Data Analytics. ECON CORE COURSES – 12 Credit Hours ECON 5302: Microeconomic Theory The determination of prices and output. The theory of markets ranging from perfect competition through monopolistic competition and oligopoly to monopoly. The theory of the firm and the industry. Welfare implications of the price determination. ECON 5303: Macroeconomic Theory The analysis of the determination of total income in the economy and related problems. Strong emphasis is given to the theory of money, and the relationship between government policy and economic activity. ECON 5305: Applied Mathematical Economics A problem-oriented survey of the mathematical techniques utilized in economics, finance, and public policy analysis. Topics to be covered include matrix algebra, optimization problems, comparative statics, dynamics, and game theory. The purpose of the course is to introduce students to the quantitative methods necessary to understand the current literature in Economics and Finance. ECON 5370: Applied Econometrics Linear and nonlinear regression methodologies, elementary time series analysis, and other introductory econometric topics will be treated. The course is designed to provide basic expertise in the application of econometric techniques to hypothesis testing, model building, diagnostic testing, and simulation analysis. Advanced Econometrics and Applied Courses –  9 Credit Hours ECON 5371: Econometric Forecasting Econometric Forecasting A survey of univariate time series, single equation, and multi-equation systems approaches to applied econometric forecasting analysis. Topics to be covered include autoregressive-moving storage average (ARIMA) modeling, model identification, estimation, diagnostic checking and out-of-sample simulation. Applications will be drawn from Latin American business conditions, exchange rate, inflation, national income, and balance of payments forecasting methods. ECON 5372: Panel Data & Discrete Choice Models This course provides an introduction to panel data plus binary and multiple-outcome qualitative response econometric methods. Panel topics to be covered are fixed effects and random-effects. Discrete choice qualitative response topics to be covered include linear probability, probit, logit, ordered logit, nested logit, and multinomial logit models. ECON 5330 – Economic and Financial Applications of Python Programming Conferences and discussions of various topics in mathematics and statistics by faculty, graduate students, and outside speakers. Required of all graduate students during each semester of full-time enrollment. Electives/advanced - topics courses –  9 Credit Hours Electives based on course availability Electives based on course availability DEADLINES AND APPLICATION Apply online at utep.edu/graduate . Applications must include official transcripts and statement of purpose. Fall Term US: July 15 Mexican: May 1 Other International: April 1 Spring Term US: November 15 Mexican: October 1 Other International: September 1 Summer Term US: May 1 Mexican: February 1 Other International: January 1 TUITION In-state tuition and fees total $11,334.05* Cost of out-of-state (international) students is approximately 2.25x the in-state cost. *Note: Cost estimates are subject to change each academic year. CONTACT John Gibson, Ph.D. MS Economics Coordinator Business Room 226 915-747-5435 jdgibson@utep.edu <!--\n    .accWrapper > div {\n    padding-left:24px;\n    }\n    .collapseToggleControl.collapsed span.glyphicon:before {\n        cont