{"url": "https://www.utep.edu/programs/graduate/nursing-admin-management-msn.html", "title": "Nursing Administration and Management MSN", "text": "College of Nursing Nursing Administration and Management, MSN The Master of Science in Nursing Administration and Management program prepares advanced-practice registered nurses for leadership and management roles in a variety of health care settings. Courses are delivered online for maximum flexibility. Students explore key administration concepts in human resources, health care finance and quality management. A bachelor’s degree in nursing and U.S. RN license are required to apply. .program-overview-section .w-col-5 {\n\tdisplay: none;\n}\n.program-overview-section .w-col.w-col-7 {\n    margin: 0 auto;\n    width: 960px;\n}\n@media screen and (max-width: 991px) {\n\t.program-overview-section .w-col.w-col-7 {\n\t    width: 100%;\n\t}\n}\n.pp-wrapper {\n    background-position: 50% 0;\n} Request info Accredited by Commission on Collegiate Nursing Education Eligible students admitted into the program can apply for the HCA Healthcare Nursing Fellowship Finish in two years for a total of 33 credit hours + 90-hour mentored residency experience MSN Helped CNO Advance Her Career UTEP opened the door for Annie L. Garcia to step into her role as chief nursing officer at Del Sol Medical Center in El Paso. Annie began her nursing career in 2003 and she credits her graduate education for helping to advance her career at HCA, the largest hospital operator in the U.S. Before serving as Del Sol's top nursing executive, Annie was CNO at Methodist Texsan Hospital in San Antonio. Annie L. Garcia MSN 2013 Career Opportunities A degree in Nursing Administration and Management can lead to career opportunities in administration, management or leadership in hospitals, physicians’ offices and residential care facilities. Chief Nursing Officer Nurse Administrator Nurse Manager .program-hero-section {\n    background-image: url(_images/nursing-ad